  "notes": "Commons has only right-side photos (VT-BWH, VT-BXY, 2024); the left side is taken to be the same with the fin mirrored, titles in the same place. Fin tail art differs between aircraft (several textile patterns/colors); the ribbons and orange/teal tail cone look common to all. The A320/A321 fleet taken over from AirAsia India and Air India carries the same scheme. Older 737-800s may still wear the pre-2023 Air India Express scheme (white with orange/red tail art of Indian landmarks) until repainted. Belly 'AIX' only glimpsed from a steep angle. Re-checked 2026-11-15 on VT-BWH and VT-BXY (both from ahead, so placed between the forward door and the overwing exits, whose places on the 737-8 are known): 'express' runs about x 0.154 to 0.371 (it had been drawn starting 0.9 m further forward and running past the exits); it has no ascenders, so its height is its lowercase's (xHeight 1, height 0.6, the lowercase as first drawn); 'AIR INDIA' sits over its aft half, x 0.262 to 0.358, not over its start, its baseline just above the lowercase's tops. The heights are left as drawn: neither photo is near side-on. The orange belly's edge is a curve: smooth. The engine's 'AIX' length is a guess from the oblique views (low).",
  "history": "Replaced Skyarium v1's approximation. Introduced 2023-10 with the AIX rebrand (Air India Express + AIX Connect, ex-AirAsia India, merged 2024-10); replaced the 2005-2023 white scheme with landmark tail art."
